      "weight": "strong",
      "why": "Guideline E concerns arise from the Individual’s deliberate omission and false statements on the QNSP and failure to disclose cocaine use; the Judge found these omissions unmitigated under the adjudicative guidelines."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "facts": ["F4", "F5", "F6", "F7", "F8"],
      "equity": "due_process",
      "favors": ["O3"],
      "weight": "strong",
      "why": "Guideline G concerns (excessive alcohol use and DUI) were not mitigated because the Individual did not demonstrate a clear, sustained pattern of abstinence or compliance with treatment recommendations."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "facts": ["F3", "F6", "F8"],
      "equity": "institutional_credibility",
      "favors": ["O3"],
      "weight": "strong",
      "why": "Guideline H concerns (illegal drug use) remain because the Individual denied cocaine use despite medical records, offered no evidence of abstinence, and failed to satisfy any mitigating condition for drug misuse."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "facts": ["F2"],
      "equity": "lawful_authority",
      "favors": ["O3"],
      "weight": "strong",
      "why": "Regulatory presumption against granting or restoring a clearance requires the individual to overcome the security concerns; the Judge concluded the burden was not met."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "facts": ["F8"],
      "equity": "record_integrity",
      "favors": ["O3"],
      "weight": "weak",
      "why": "The psychologist’s assessment of under‑reporting undermines the Individual’s credibility, further weighing against mitigation."
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": {
    "read": "The case centers on whether the Individual has sufficiently remedied the security concerns raised under Guidelines E (lack of candor), G (alcohol misuse and DUI), and H (cocaine use). The Judge applied the whole‑person concept and the statutory presumption that doubts must be resolved in favor of national security.",
    "weighing": "The Judge found that none of the mitigating conditions listed in the adjudicative guidelines were satisfied. For Guideline G, the Individual’s intermittent sobriety and relapse history failed to meet the clear‑abstinence standard (p13.4‑p13.6). For Guideline H, the lack of acknowledgment of cocaine use and absence of a drug‑treatment program meant no mitigation (p14.4‑p14.5). For Guideline E, the false statements on the QNSP and failure to report drug use were unmitigated (p15.2‑p15.4). Because the regulatory presumption against restoration remains unshifted, the balance tips toward denial.",
    "tradecraft": "The decision will be entered as a final Administrative Judge Decision, recorded in the DOE security file, and communicated to the LSO and the Individual with reference to 10 C.F.R. § 710.7(a). The denial is irreversible and may be appealed under 10 C.F.R. § 710.28.",
    "uncertainty": "The primary uncertainty lies in the Individual’s future behavior; however, the record as of the hearing date does not demonstrate sufficient rehabilitation or reformation.",
    "handoff": "The Administrative Judge acts alone under statutory authority; no further inter‑agency consultation is required.",
    "tempting_wrong": {
      "option": "O4",
      "pull": "The Individual’s recent negative PEth tests and participation in AA may appear to show recovery, tempting a grant.",
      "why_wrong": "The record shows continued under‑reporting, lack of a sustained 12‑month abstinence period, and unresolved drug‑use concerns, which the regulations expressly require before clearance can be restored."
    },
    "rejected": {
      "O1": "The LSO’s evidence is already on the record; requesting additional proof would be unnecessary and contrary to the Judge’s duty to decide based on the existing file.",
      "O2": "Holding the decision pending further sobriety evidence would conflict with the statutory presumption against granting and the Judge’s finding that the required mitigating conditions are not met."
    }
  },
  "verdict": {
    "choice": "O3",
    "basis": "settled",
    "decisive": ["C1", "C2", "C3", "C4"]
  },
  "basis_statement": "I conclude that the Individual’s security clearance should not be restored.",
  "flip_conditions": {
    "would_change": [
      {
        "id": "flip1",
        "if": "The Individual could demonstrate a continuous twelve‑month period of abstinence from alcohol, verified by monthly PEth testing and a favorable prognosis from a qualified professional.",
        "then": "O4",
        "why": "This would satisfy mitigating condition (b) for Guideline G, showing a clear and established pattern of abstinence.",
        "origin": "stated",
        "spans": ["p13.4", "p13.6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "flip2",
        "if": "The Individual fully disclosed his cocaine use, completed a prescribed drug‑treatment program, and provided evidence of sustained abstinence.",
        "then": "O4",
        "why": "This would meet mitigating conditions (b) and (d) for Guideline H and also address Guideline E’s candor concerns.",
        "origin": "stated",
        "spans": ["p14.4", "p15.2"]
      }
    ],
    "would_not_change": [
      {
        "id": "noflip1",
        "if": "The DUI charge is dismissed, as it already was.",
        "why": "The underlying alcohol‑misuse behavior and the DUI incident remain security concerns under Guideline G regardless of criminal outcome.",
        "origin": "authored",
        "spans": []
      }
    ],
    "watch_for": [
      {
        "id": "watch1",
        "what": "Any future positive drug test or relapse into alcohol misuse.",
        "favors": ["O3"],
        "why": "A relapse would reinforce the finding that the Individual has not mitigated the security concerns.",
        "origin": "authored",
        "spans": []
      }
    ]
  },
  "lesson": "Under DOE Part 710, a security clearance cannot be restored unless the individual fully mitigates each adjudicative guideline concern; partial treatment or intermittent negative tests are insufficient without clear, sustained abstinence and full candor. The whole‑person assessment demands evidence that the risk of unreliability is no longer present.",
  "limits": "This reasoning applies only to cases where the record shows unresolved drug use, false statements, and insufficient evidence of sustained sobriety. It does not extend to situations where the individual provides conclusive, documented proof of rehabilitation that meets the specific mitigating conditions.",
  "authored": [
    "reasoning.tradecraft",
    "reasoning.tempting_wrong",
    "lesson",
    "limits",
    "reasoning.handoff"
  ],
  "equity_gaps": []
}

